  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1..A stratus deck along a stationary back door cold\nfront is spread through much of the region. Just south of this\nstratus deck, clear skies, low level moisture, and light to calm\nwinds is providing ideal conditions for fog formation.\nObservations have consistently been at 1/4 miles in Onslow,\nDuplin, and southern Craven counties. While no observation sites\nare in the area, it is likely patchy dense fog is also occuring\nin Jones and Carteret counties. A SPS is in effect for patchy\ndense fog this morning in the aforementioned counties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Back door cold front shifts back north today and\nflow becomes southwesterly through the CWA. If the stratus\nbreaks up early in the morning, we should have another near\nrecord warm day for ENC as temps soar into the upper 80s inland.  \n(record high climate section below). However, if the cloud cover\nthis morning lingers, we could have a delayed start to the\nheating and temps could max out in the mid 80s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cold front will be passing through the region Saturday,\nstalling offshore along the Gulf Stream. A coastal low forming \noffshore along this front is becoming more likely, but there is\nstill high uncertainty on where/when the low forms, how deep it\ngets, and where it tracks. While there are still three main \ncamps as far as solutions go, confidence is trending up that a \ncoastal low will form.\n\nOnce the low forms out over the gulf stream there are three main\nsteering paths that models are hinting at right now. The most\nlikely scenario at this time remains that the low forms and \nstays over the gulf stream picking up strengths as it does so \nbut remains in a fairly constant longitudinal range offshore.\nThis will result in minor to low end moderate impacts to our NC\ncoastline, as strong NE winds paired with a low deepening\noffshore bring minor to low-end moderate coastal flooding,\nbeach erosion, and overwash concerns. \n\nThe next likely outcome is the low develops right along the \ncoast and holds it there for a much more prolonged event. Should\nthis solution play out, we could be looking at a long duration \nof strong NE winds and high seas. This outcome would result in\nmoderate impacts to the NC coastline. In addition, the track of\nthe low would have to be monitored in case it shifts back west \ntowards eastern NC. There is still considerable spread in the \nlocation and intensity details for models showing this outcome.\n\nFinally, the least likely outcome is the low either not \ndevelops at all or develops it much later and much further east\nthan any of the other scenarios. With a strong high building \nin, we would still see strong NE winds, albeit for a shorter \nduration, and elevated waves bringing minor coastal impacts.\n\nRegardless of which scenario plays out, there is HIGH confidence\nof marine impacts, and potential for waves 8+ ft reaching\nportions of the OBX. A High Surf Advisory is in effect for\nHatteras Island and northern OBX. Winds advisory potential \ncontinues for the OBX zones, as well as perhaps portions of the \nadjacent coastal mainland. Will have to watch for minor coastal \nflood concerns for srn portions of Pamlico Sound and the coastal\nareas of the OBX with the sustained NE flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Coolest and driest airmass for the fall season \nthus far builds in behind the front Sat night and beyond with \ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the \n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). However, depending how offshore \nthe front makes it, could keep things a bit milder than fcst if \nsome clouds advect in from offshore keeping the nighttime mins \na bit warmer than advertised.",
